# DeFi Exploit Lab

Real smart-contract exploit classes, each reproduced as a **minimal, runnable
Foundry PoC**: a vulnerable contract, the attacker that breaks it, the fix, and a
test that proves the attack works *and* that the fix stops it.

The goal is not to collect vulnerabilities. It is to show the reasoning an auditor
applies — where the bug hides, why it survives review, and what class of fix
actually closes it rather than just moving the symptom.

Every exploit and every fix is asserted in code. `forge test` is the proof.

```bash
git clone --recursive https://github.com/Arashrahmaniii/defi-exploit-lab.git
cd defi-exploit-lab
forge test -vv
```

```
14 tests passed, 0 failed  —  4 exploit classes, each with exploit + fix + fuzz
```

---

## The exploits

### 01 · Single-function reentrancy — *The DAO, 2016 (~3.6M ETH)*

[`src/01-reentrancy`](src/01-reentrancy/ReentrantVault.sol) ·
[test](test/01_Reentrancy.t.sol)

The original. A vault sends ETH before zeroing the caller's balance, so a contract
recipient re-enters `withdraw` from its `receive` hook while its credit still
reads as unspent. The PoC drains a vault holding two other users' deposits with a
1-ETH stake, then shows that either checks-effects-interactions *or* a reentrancy
guard closes it — and includes both, because CEI is easy to break in a later
refactor and the guard is a cheap invariant that survives one.

**Heuristic:** read every external call as "arbitrary code runs here" and ask
which invariants are false at that instant.

### 02 · Read-only reentrancy — *Sentiment 2023 (~$1M), dForce 2023 (~$3.6M)*

[`src/02-readonly-reentrancy`](src/02-readonly-reentrancy/SharePool.sol) ·
[test](test/02_ReadOnlyReentrancy.t.sol)

The one most teams still miss. A pool's `pricePerShare()` is a `view`, and it is
correct at every point an integrator can observe it — *except* from inside the
pool's own withdrawal callback, where the balance has already dropped but the
share supply has not. A separate lending market that prices collateral off that
read liquidates a **perfectly healthy** borrower during the window.

The two contracts are in different files on purpose: the vulnerable pool never
loses a wei, and the lending market has no bug of its own. The loss is *exported*.
The fix guards the `view` itself — a `nonReentrant` modifier on the mutating
functions does nothing here.

**Heuristic:** for every public view that combines two or more storage reads, ask
whether any external call sits *between* the writes to those slots.

### 03 · ERC-4626 inflation / first-depositor attack — *Cream, Hundred Finance*

[`src/03-vault-inflation`](src/03-vault-inflation/Vault.sol) ·
[test](test/03_VaultInflation.t.sol)

A vault whose `totalAssets()` is its token balance can be inflated by a direct
transfer that mints no shares. The attacker mints 1 wei of shares, donates enough
to make that share worth more than the victim's whole deposit, and the victim's
share calculation floors to zero — tokens in, no claim out. The PoC steals the
first depositor's entire balance. The fix is OpenZeppelin's virtual
shares/assets, and the fuzz test states the real invariant: an accepted deposit
always mints shares, and a rejected one takes nothing — the vault never silently
confiscates.

**Heuristic:** any vault where `totalAssets()` is `balanceOf(this)` — ask what
happens when supply is 1 and someone transfers in directly.

### 04 · Flash-loan oracle manipulation — *bZx, Harvest 2020 (~$34M)*

[`src/04-oracle-manipulation`](src/04-oracle-manipulation/MoneyMarket.sol) ·
[test](test/04_OracleManipulation.t.sol)

The dominant exploit class of 2020–2022. A money market prices collateral off an
AMM's spot reserve ratio; an attacker flash-borrows, swaps to spike that ratio,
borrows against the inflated valuation, unwinds the swap, and repays the loan — all
in one transaction, leaving the price exactly where it started. The PoC draws 4×
the honest credit limit against unchanged collateral.

The fix is not a patch to the market — the market logic is fine. It is a different
**price source**: the same contract wired to a 30-minute TWAP is not exploitable
in one transaction, because a same-block round trip contributes zero elapsed time
to a time-weighted average. The test proves the identical attack reverts under the
TWAP while honest borrowing still works.

**Heuristic:** trace every price to its origin. If it resolves to `reserveX /
reserveY` on a pool an attacker can trade against, assume it can be set to any
value for the length of one transaction.

---

## Why this repo exists

Static analysis finds none of these. Slither does not flag read-only reentrancy,
does not know a spot price is manipulable, and does not reason about first-
depositor rounding. They are found by a person tracing value and control flow —
which is the skill this lab demonstrates.

Each PoC is deliberately reduced to the essential shape of the bug: no proxies, no
upgradeability, no unrelated protocol surface. A reviewer can read one exploit and
its fix in a few minutes and leave understanding the class, not just the instance.

## Layout

```
src/
  01-reentrancy/          ReentrantVault.sol       vulnerable + fixed + attacker
  02-readonly-reentrancy/ SharePool.sol            pool with a manipulable view
                          LendingDesk.sol          the integrator that loses money
  03-vault-inflation/     Vault.sol                vulnerable + virtual-shares fix
  04-oracle-manipulation/ AMM.sol                  constant-product pool + TWAP
                          MoneyMarket.sol           spot-priced vs TWAP-priced
  mocks/                  MockERC20.sol
test/
  01..04_*.t.sol          exploit + fix + fuzz for each class
```

## Roadmap

Next classes to add, in the order they show up in real audits: signature replay /
missing nonce, unchecked `delegatecall` / storage-collision in proxies,
approval-race and fee-on-transfer accounting, and precision-loss griefing in
reward distributors.

## Disclaimer

Every contract here is intentionally vulnerable and exists only to be attacked in
a test harness. Nothing in this repository should be deployed or used with real
funds.




### 📦 Source Code Files (Foundry / Exploit PoC / Source):

File: `src/01-reentrancy/ReentrantVault.sol`
